  "summary": "Recent work recasts species tree and rooted-tree partitioning tasks as tractable problems on quantum hardware and at million-taxon scale.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these items is reformulating core phylogenetic tree tasks as computationally tractable problems. QUANTA maps weighted quartet bipartitioning to a QUBO problem solved by quantum annealing on D-Wave hardware for species tree estimation. QRAFT pursues scale within a statistically consistent coalescent framework, inferring million-taxon trees with near-linear time and linear memory. PhytClust exactly optimizes monophyletic partitioning of rooted trees without a threshold and selects cluster number automatically. Ok-k-mer addresses genomic distance estimation by selecting weighted k-mer length ensembles that maximize Spearman correlation with phylogenetic branch distances. What is new is the pairing of exact or statistically consistent formulations with new hardware and scale targets, rather than a shared algorithm.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-10"
}
